done 10. include the soft surface algorithm. Briefly, in transform_curves, if soft =T, then probe_sensitivity_external need to be assigned as either a numeric value or a character (column name in metadata). The function will copy that value or the designated column to a new column with standardized name probe_true_sensitivity_V_nm_<useCurve>. Then, during curve transformation, 1) the analyze_baseline will take this probe_true_sensitivity as its input, as opposed to the sensitivity value defined with the curve. This value only influences the filtering criteria for removing curves with undulating baseline (std and slp is calculated with force instead of raw deflection value), but will not influence the baseline value (the average of defelection of last N points). Note that analyze_baseline() is edited to include soft and external_sensitivity_column arguments. 2) before running transform_one (the wrapper of transform_a_curve), if soft=T, probe_sensitivity_vec will be assigned values with the value in the probe_true_sensitivity_V_nm_<useCurve>. This value will be inserted to transform_one as the other values for each curve iteratively. Transform_one will call transform_a_curve, which has been updated so that 1) takes soft and probe_sensitivity arguments. if soft, it will use probe_sensitivity as opposed to the sensitivity defined with the given curve for tranforming deflection to force_nN. The operation on x-axis (i.e., tranform piezo moving distance to separation distance), is not affected, so that no matter if soft is True or False, it is always transformed using the sensitivity defined with the given curve. This function is checked with test_softsurface_option.R under curvana_test.
